$BODY = <<<'HTML'
<h2>The dispute</h2>
<p>The respondent served as a constable and retired on 31 December 2022. His pension and retiral dues were then withheld, and a recovery of Rs 23,08,264 was demanded — the alleged excess of salary paid to him over the years. The Public Services Tribunal (Claim Petition No. 94/NB/DB/2023) found for him on 20 December 2024, setting aside the recovery order of 1 June 2023 and directing release of his dues within sixty days. The State&rsquo;s writ petition against that judgment came before a Division Bench of Hon&rsquo;ble the Acting Chief Justice Sri Manoj Kumar Tiwari and Hon&rsquo;ble Sri Justice Subhash Upadhyay.</p>

<h2>The State&rsquo;s argument — and why it failed</h2>
<p>The State pointed to the respondent&rsquo;s disciplinary history — nineteen punishments between 1997 and 2003 — and argued that he knew he was not entitled to the excess salary yet never informed the department, making him equally responsible for the overpayment. The Bench was unpersuaded. The pleadings contained no allegation of fraud or misrepresentation, no claim that the employee engineered the fixation, and no undertaking by him to refund excess amounts. Absent those elements, <em>Rafiq Masih</em> governs: recovery from a retired employee of amounts paid through the employer&rsquo;s own error is impermissible.</p>
<div class="compare">
  <div class="col old"><strong>Recovery permissible</strong><br>Where the employee committed fraud or misrepresentation to obtain the payment, or gave an undertaking to refund excess amounts, or holds a position outside the protected categories with recovery sought promptly.</div>
  <div class="arrow">→</div>
  <div class="col new"><strong>Recovery impermissible</strong><br>From retired employees or those about to retire, and from low-paid categories, where the excess flowed from the employer&rsquo;s own fixation error and the employee did nothing to cause it.</div>
</div>

<h2>The operative outcome</h2>
<p>The writ petition was disposed of without interfering with the Tribunal&rsquo;s view. The competent authority may refix the respondent&rsquo;s pay and pension — the respondent had no objection to prospective correction — and must release all retiral dues within ten weeks of presentation of the certified copy of the order.</p>

<h2>The principle&rsquo;s practical reach</h2>
<p>Recovery disputes of this kind recur across government departments, municipal bodies and public undertakings, usually triggered by audit objections years after the event. The structure of the law is employee-protective but not absolute: it distinguishes the innocent recipient of a departmental mistake from the employee who procured the payment. Two corollaries matter in practice. First, the department&rsquo;s remedy against its own error is prospective refixation, not retrospective clawback from a pensioner&rsquo;s gratuity. Second, employees should be cautious about signing refund undertakings at the time of provisional fixations or promotions, because an undertaking is precisely what converts an impermissible recovery into a permissible one.</p>
<div class="check">
  <p>Retirees facing recovery notices should ask three questions: Is fraud or misrepresentation actually alleged? Was any undertaking given? Does the demand reach back years into service? The answers usually decide the case.</p>
  <p>Departments should route audit-flagged fixation errors into prospective correction orders, reasoned and communicated, rather than omnibus recovery demands at retirement.</p>
</div>
<div class="note">This article reports a judgment for general information. Recovery questions depend on the employee&rsquo;s category, the cause of the excess payment and any undertakings on record.</div>
